1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:02,830
Every photograph tells a story. We see

2
00:00:02,830 --> 00:00:05,790
mountains, lights, shadows, and

3
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:08,110
countless tiny details. But to a

4
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:10,590
computer, an image is not really a

5
00:00:10,590 --> 00:00:12,830
photograph. It is a collection of

6
00:00:12,830 --> 00:00:15,510
numbers. Every pixel stores numerical

7
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:17,630
information, and when millions of these

8
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:19,950
pixels come together, they create the

9
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:22,430
images we see every day. Now,

10
00:00:22,510 --> 00:00:24,670
imagine if we could represent an entire

11
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:26,750
photograph using significantly fewer

12
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:29,340
numberswithout losing most of what makes

13
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:31,900
it recognizable. Would that be possible?

14
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:34,660
Surprisingly, the answer comes from

15
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:37,500
mathematics. More specifically, from the

16
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:39,580
fascinating world of linear algebra,

17
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:42,460
where matrices, eigenvectors, and

18
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:44,540
eigenvalues can help us understand how

19
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:47,180
information is structured. Today,

20
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:49,740
let's uncover the mathematics behind

21
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:50,780
image compression.

22
00:00:51,158 --> 00:00:53,078
Let's start with how a computer

23
00:00:53,078 --> 00:00:55,838
understands an image. An image is made-up

24
00:00:55,838 --> 00:00:58,518
of tiny elements called pixels. Each

25
00:00:58,518 --> 00:01:00,678
pixel contains numerical values

26
00:01:00,678 --> 00:01:03,158
representing its brightness and color. To

27
00:01:03,158 --> 00:01:06,118
simplify our calculations, we convert

28
00:01:06,118 --> 00:01:08,838
our photograph into grayscale. Now,

29
00:01:08,998 --> 00:01:10,958
every pixel can be represented by a

30
00:01:10,958 --> 00:01:13,718
single intensity value, instead of three

31
00:01:13,718 --> 00:01:16,218
separate color channels. We can arrange

32
00:01:16,218 --> 00:01:18,658
these values into a rectangular matrix,

33
00:01:18,938 --> 00:01:21,938
which we'll call A. In our example, the

34
00:01:21,938 --> 00:01:24,298
matrix has 180 rows and

35
00:01:24,298 --> 00:01:26,978
320 columns. That gives us a

36
00:01:26,978 --> 00:01:29,538
total of 57,600

37
00:01:29,538 --> 00:01:32,338
numerical values. So, instead of

38
00:01:32,338 --> 00:01:34,658
working with a photograph, we are now

39
00:01:34,658 --> 00:01:37,098
working with a mathematical object. And

40
00:01:37,098 --> 00:01:38,818
this is where something interesting

41
00:01:38,818 --> 00:01:39,378
happens.

42
00:01:39,778 --> 00:01:41,778
Can we find a simpler mathematical

43
00:01:41,778 --> 00:01:44,378
representation of this matrix, one that

44
00:01:44,378 --> 00:01:46,058
captures most of its important

45
00:01:46,058 --> 00:01:48,978
information?To answer that, we first

46
00:01:48,978 --> 00:01:51,018
need to understand egenvectors and

47
00:01:51,018 --> 00:01:52,098
diagonalization.

48
00:01:52,496 --> 00:01:54,835
Our image matrix A is rectangular.

49
00:01:55,137 --> 00:01:57,326
Ordinary eigenvalue diagonalization

50
00:01:57,326 --> 00:01:59,892
applies to square matrices, so we cannot

51
00:01:59,892 --> 00:02:02,194
directly diagonalize this image matrix in

52
00:02:02,194 --> 00:02:05,024
the usual way. Instead, we construct a

53
00:02:05,024 --> 00:02:07,892
new matrix by multiplying a transpose by

54
00:02:08,269 --> 00:02:10,156
A. An eigenvector represents a special

55
00:02:10,156 --> 00:02:12,081
direction that remains unchanged in

56
00:02:12,081 --> 00:02:14,307
direction when a matrix transformation is

57
00:02:14,307 --> 00:02:16,515
applied. It may only be stretched,

58
00:02:16,741 --> 00:02:18,930
compressed, or reversed. The

59
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:20,817
corresponding eigenvalue tells us the

60
00:02:20,817 --> 00:02:23,081
factor by which that direction is scaled.

61
00:02:23,458 --> 00:02:25,760
Mathematically, we can express the

62
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:28,741
decomposition as: A transpose A equals

63
00:02:28,741 --> 00:02:31,609
V lambda V transpose. But what does this

64
00:02:31,609 --> 00:02:34,250
actually mean?Think of it as changing our

65
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:37,213
coordinate system. In this new basis,the

66
00:02:37,213 --> 00:02:40,118
matrix becomes diagonal. This reveals the

67
00:02:40,118 --> 00:02:41,213
underlying structure of the

68
00:02:41,213 --> 00:02:43,477
transformation and leads us directly to

69
00:02:43,477 --> 00:02:44,534
our next concept

70
00:02:44,867 --> 00:02:46,848
This brings us to singular value

71
00:02:46,848 --> 00:02:49,438
decomposition. SVD allows us to

72
00:02:49,438 --> 00:02:51,952
decompose our original matrix into three

73
00:02:51,952 --> 00:02:54,695
simpler matrices: U, Sigma,

74
00:02:54,924 --> 00:02:57,667
and V transpose. Each matrix plays a

75
00:02:57,667 --> 00:03:00,409
different role. V transpose represents

76
00:03:00,409 --> 00:03:03,114
the input directions. Sigma contains the

77
00:03:03,114 --> 00:03:05,286
singular values, which describe how

78
00:03:05,286 --> 00:03:08,009
strongly each component is scaled. and u

79
00:03:08,009 --> 00:03:09,990
represents the corresponding output

80
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:12,124
directions. An important connection

81
00:03:12,124 --> 00:03:14,600
exists between eigenvalues and singular

82
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:16,848
values. The singular values are the

83
00:03:16,848 --> 00:03:18,752
square roots of the eigenvalues of A

84
00:03:18,752 --> 00:03:19,476
transpose

85
00:03:19,476 --> 00:03:22,333
A. In other words, lambda i equals

86
00:03:22,333 --> 00:03:25,076
sigma i squared. These singular values

87
00:03:25,076 --> 00:03:27,209
are arranged from largest to smallest.

88
00:03:27,590 --> 00:03:29,571
The largest values generally represent

89
00:03:29,571 --> 00:03:31,324
the strongest components of the image.

90
00:03:31,686 --> 00:03:33,324
while smaller values contribute

91
00:03:33,324 --> 00:03:35,609
progressively less. And this gives us a

92
00:03:35,609 --> 00:03:38,543
powerful idea. What if we simply removed

93
00:03:38,543 --> 00:03:40,790
some of the smaller components?Could we

94
00:03:40,790 --> 00:03:42,619
still reconstruct an image that looks

95
00:03:42,619 --> 00:03:43,990
almost like the original?

96
00:03:44,318 --> 00:03:46,528
This is where image compression happens.

97
00:03:46,908 --> 00:03:49,118
Instead of keeping every singular value,

98
00:03:49,423 --> 00:03:51,823
we retain only the largest k values and

99
00:03:51,823 --> 00:03:53,766
their corresponding vectors. This

100
00:03:53,766 --> 00:03:55,937
technique is called truncated singular

101
00:03:55,937 --> 00:03:58,794
value decomposition. Our approximation is

102
00:03:58,794 --> 00:04:01,613
represented by uk, sigmak

103
00:04:02,147 --> 00:04:04,966
and vk transpose. Let's see what

104
00:04:04,966 --> 00:04:07,566
happens when we change k. With just one

105
00:04:07,566 --> 00:04:09,547
component, we capture the broadest

106
00:04:09,547 --> 00:04:11,756
structure of the image, but lose most of

107
00:04:11,756 --> 00:04:14,385
its fine details. With five components,

108
00:04:14,689 --> 00:04:17,013
more shapes and visual features begin to

109
00:04:17,013 --> 00:04:17,432
appear.

110
00:04:17,773 --> 00:04:20,653
At rank 10, the reconstruction becomes

111
00:04:20,653 --> 00:04:23,213
much more recognizable. And with 20

112
00:04:23,213 --> 00:04:25,853
components, we recover a surprisingly

113
00:04:25,853 --> 00:04:28,013
detailed approximation of the original

114
00:04:28,013 --> 00:04:30,673
photograph. Now, let's look at the

115
00:04:30,673 --> 00:04:33,473
numbers. Our original matrix contains

116
00:04:33,473 --> 00:04:35,393
57,600

117
00:04:35,393 --> 00:04:38,353
values. But with a rank 20 approximation,

118
00:04:38,673 --> 00:04:41,193
we only need 10,020 values to

119
00:04:41,193 --> 00:04:43,233
represent the image using the truncated

120
00:04:43,233 --> 00:04:45,393
factors. That's approximately

121
00:04:45,393 --> 00:04:48,033
82.6% fewer numerical

122
00:04:48,033 --> 00:04:50,382
values. And the reconstruction retains

123
00:04:50,593 --> 00:04:53,433
approximately 97.3% of the

124
00:04:53,433 --> 00:04:54,805
original image's energy.

125
00:04:55,152 --> 00:04:58,032
we can preserve much of the

126
00:04:58,032 --> 00:04:59,832
image's important structure while

127
00:04:59,832 --> 00:05:01,912
representing it with far fewer numbers.

128
00:05:02,302 --> 00:05:04,102
We began with a photograph and

129
00:05:04,102 --> 00:05:06,862
transformed it into a matrix. Using

130
00:05:06,862 --> 00:05:09,702
egenvectors and diagonalization, we

131
00:05:09,702 --> 00:05:11,822
explored its mathematical structure.

132
00:05:12,142 --> 00:05:14,062
Then, through singular value

133
00:05:14,062 --> 00:05:16,382
decomposition, we identified the

134
00:05:16,382 --> 00:05:18,342
components that contribute most to the

135
00:05:18,342 --> 00:05:20,662
image. By keeping only the most

136
00:05:20,662 --> 00:05:23,102
significant components, we created a

137
00:05:23,102 --> 00:05:25,502
low-rank approximation. And that's the

138
00:05:25,502 --> 00:05:27,542
fascinating connection between linear

139
00:05:27,542 --> 00:05:30,142
algebra and image compression. Less

140
00:05:30,142 --> 00:05:33,142
data, nearly the same picture. That's

141
00:05:33,142 --> 00:05:35,582
the mathematics behind image compression.
